  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>At the foot of the Maures massif, a few minutes from Port Grimaud and the Gulf of Saint-Tropez, Ranch La Mène blends several worlds: an educational mini-farm where young and old can feed ponies, goats, sheep, pigs, llamas and poultry, a riding centre with horse rides and pony introduction, and a western-style restaurant, Le Grill du Ranch, serving wood-fired grilled meats in a warm, family atmosphere.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The tourist office listings say "pets accepted". The ranch's official website, however, publishes no written rule on dogs, and it is not known exactly where dogs are admitted (farm, restaurant): ask on site. We advise keeping your dog on a leash, simply as a precaution, since the site also hosts farm animals roaming semi-freely (ponies, goats, poultry...) that an over-curious dog might excite.</p>
      <p>For the mini-farm, entry is €2 for an adult, €5 for a child over 3 and €3 below that (official website, read 06/11/2026); payment is by cash, cheque or holiday vouchers at the ranch and farm, and by card too at the restaurant. The Gulf of Saint-Tropez tourist office lists Le Grill du Ranch (125 seats plus 180 on the terrace, air-conditioned) as "pets accepted", like the ranch itself, without distinguishing dining room and terrace. The hours published by the website and by the tourist office differ a little by season (see the Hours line): a call before coming remains the best option, on 06 11 81 11 32, the number published by the ranch.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: I love this kind of place — you get to sniff the ponies and goats on a leash while my humans enjoy the grill.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Port Grimaud and its canals, as well as the Gulf of Saint-Tropez, are a few minutes' drive away. The village of Grimaud, with its citadel and Provençal lanes, rounds off a day in the area nicely.</p>
